From 8491e9ef76afc93a36cd0aa8f03689b074ee5dd4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jordan Bettcher <thebettcher@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 17 Sep 2026 04:49:01 -0600
Subject: [PATCH v2 18/21] Syscalls: return EFAULT for bad guest path, utsname
 and sigset pointers

Several shared syscall handlers dereference guest pointers in FEX's own C++
code, so a bad pointer kills FEX with a host fault in the syscall's
deferred-signal section, where Linux returns EFAULT. The VerifyIs* helpers
some of them call are no-ops in release builds (they only abort on NULL).

Trigger: any guest syscall with an unmapped pointer, e.g. from a static
x86-64 test:
  uname((void *)1)
  rt_sigprocmask(SIG_BLOCK, (void *)8, NULL, 8)
  openat(AT_FDCWD, (void *)1, O_RDONLY)
Before, each died with "FEX: FATAL host fault: signal 11 ... raised in a
deferred-signal section" (the openat one in strlen under the FileManager).
After, each returns -1/EFAULT, as it does on Linux (the same test on an
aarch64 Raspberry Pi 5).

Changes:
- FaultSafeUserMemAccess::CopyStringFromUser, which is strncpy_from_user
  semantics and copies up to each 4K boundary so a string that ends just
  before an unmapped page still reads. There are also ReadFromUser and
  WriteToUser helpers.
- GuestPath copies a path argument into host memory before anything looks
  at it, like getname(): EFAULT for a bad or NULL pointer, ENAMETOOLONG at
  PATH_MAX. NULL keeps its meaning where the syscall defines one
  (utimensat, newfstatat, statx, *xattrat). It is used by every
  FileManager-backed handler in Syscalls/FS.cpp, Syscalls/FD.cpp,
  x64/FD.cpp and x32/FD.cpp.
- uname fills a local utsname and copies it out.
- rt_sigprocmask, rt_sigpending, rt_sigsuspend and signalfd(4) copy the
  sigset in and out around the SignalDelegator and check sigsetsize as the
  kernel does (it was ignored).
- readlink/readlinkat of /proc/self/exe copy the path out with CopyToUser
  instead of strncpy into the guest buffer.

Relies on "SignalDelegator: return EFAULT from a faulting
FaultSafeUserMemAccess copy" for the fault to become EFAULT.

Ubuntu 24.04 bash from the RootFS still does mktemp/ln/readlink/mv/ls -R/
stat and readlink /proc/self/exe correctly.

v2, replacing held patch 0009. Review: "correct but the ppc64le copy helper
is a byte loop that copies the rest of a 4K page per path argument, about a
microsecond on every open or stat." CopyStringFromUser is now a naked
ppc64le routine: bytes until the source is 8-aligned, then 8-byte loads
with the has-zero-byte test, stopping at the NUL. An aligned load never
crosses a page, so it faults only when the page holding the next string
byte is unreadable, where the kernel's strncpy_from_user fails; its three
load sites return -EFAULT through TryHandleSafeFault (new
IsStringFaultLocation). Other hosts keep the chunked copy.

1e6 stat("/tmp") from a static x86-64 guest, 64K host, FEX_HOSTPAGEMODE=force,
CPU 4, one run each: v1 3.913 s, v2 1.287 s. stat of a path whose NUL is
the last byte before an unmapped host page: ENOENT; running into it: EFAULT
(both versions, and on Linux).
